      <h2>1. Why Online Friendships Can Feel Real</h2>

      <p>Humans have a strong need for social connection, and digital communication can provide many of the same relationship-building behaviors found in face-to-face interaction: conversation, self-disclosure, humor, emotional support, shared experiences, and regular contact.</p>

      <p>However, an online friendship is not automatically identical to an offline friendship. The communication medium changes what information people can see and how quickly they can respond. Text removes many visual and vocal cues, while voice and video can restore some of them.</p>

      <p>The important question is therefore not whether an online friendship is “real.” It is whether the people involved are developing the behaviors that normally make relationships meaningful: trust, reciprocity, consistency, respect, and emotional support.</p>

      <h3>1.1 Why Voice and Video Can Add Social Cues</h3>

      <p>Different communication formats provide different amounts of social information. Text-based conversations rely heavily on written words, while voice conversations add tone, pace, pauses, and vocal expression. Video can add facial expressions and other visible cues.</p>

      <p>These additional signals can make it easier to interpret what another person means, although they can also be misunderstood. A smile, pause, or tone of voice does not always have one universal meaning.</p>

