      <h2>3. Why Some People Find Online Friendships Easier</h2>

      <p>Online communication can reduce certain forms of social pressure, although the experience differs from person to person. Someone who feels uncomfortable with immediate face-to-face interaction may prefer having more time to think before responding or being able to choose when to participate.</p>

      <p>At the same time, online communication introduces its own challenges. Text can be misinterpreted, people may present themselves inaccurately, and the absence of physical context can make some situations harder to understand.</p>

      <h3>3.1 The Comfort of Psychological Distance</h3>

      <p>Digital communication can create a sense of distance between people. Depending on the platform and communication format, users may have more control over when they respond, how much they reveal, and whether they continue an interaction.</p>

      <p>This can make communication feel less intimidating for some people. However, psychological distance should not be confused with complete safety or privacy. Users still need to protect personal information and pay attention to how other people behave.</p>

      <h3>3.2 Conversations That Go Deeper</h3>
      <p>Many users report sharing personal stories with online friends that they’d never share in person. When you remove fear of judgment, people focus on authenticity — the real foundation of trust.</p>

      <h3>3.3 Emotional Safety Through Design</h3>
      <p>This platform’s platform is intentionally built for emotional safety. No profiles, no “likes,” no follower counts — just genuine human conversations. This simplicity keeps users focused on the person, not the performance.</p>

      <h2>Online Friendships vs. Offline Friendships</h2>

      <p>Online and offline friendships share many relationship-building behaviors, but the environment changes how those behaviors happen.</p>

